Let’s take music departments as benchmark. Those across the world’s most prestigious universities don’t seem to agree anymore on how much actual music and technical skills their graduates ought to grapple with. In places such as Stanford, Columbia, or the École Normale Supérieure, music majors retain high levels of musical theory and analysis, paired with rigorous historiographical knowledge; elsewhere, however, the requirements of what music graduates must know have been downgraded.

In April 2020, Princeton proudly announced the first major revision of its music curriculum since the late 1990s, resulting in the halving of compulsory theory courses (from four to two) and the re-organisation of the degree around “Materials and Making” and “Culture and Criticism”, a broad category, needless to say, overloaded with some good old postmodern critical theory. At Harvard, some upper-level analytical courses explicitly require no knowledge of Western notation, such as “Analyzing Popular Music after 2000” which (let’s politely call it) alternative approach to teaching is nevertheless considered valid for “journalism, music criticism, songwriting, music production, and humanistic inquiry more broadly”.

Although hard skills and repertoire-centred knowledge per se have not been abolished at the University of Oxford, equally, they are no longer difficult to avoid. Keyboard skills, for example, do not occupy a compulsory position anymore. The mandatory “Foundations in the Study of Music” (Prelims) paper devotes half its lectures to topics including “gender and sexuality”, “postcolonialism and Orientalism”, and “class and subcultures”. During the later years of the Undergraduate course (Final Honour School), the curriculum traded its older prescribed historical architecture for a more flexible arrangement of topics, critical studies, and project-based assessments. The 2027 syllabus even allows students to substitute “Musical Analysis and Criticism” with “Musical Thought and Scholarship” which lectures are focused on “Voice”, “Body”, “Race”, “Violence”, “Death”, and “Materiality”.

